      },
      "isNameUserDefined": true,
      "description": "The 3rd-largest moon of Phanes, Appianus, is a large, sparsely cratered moon with a mass roughly 2 times that of our moon. its surface features wide flats of regolith rock there are noticibly, a handfull of cracks and ridges along its surface, meaning Appianus Underwent a phase of high geologic activity before going dorment, which cleared the surface of past crater's, and this shut down of geologic activity must have happened very reccently, as if it was even a few hundred million years ago, crater's would've had time to be made by impacts. a truly odd world, and these ridges and canyons offer a dazzling view for anyone brave enough to land here, as the surface radiation here is extremely high due to Phane's radiation belts."
    },
